-- title:
--   Crossing points on the finite chart of X₁(Mp) are supersingular
-- statement:
--   Fix a prime $p$ and an integer $M \neq 0$ with $5 \le M$ and $p \nmid M$. Let $L$ be a field of characteristic zero that is a cyclotomic extension of $\mathbb{Q}$ of order $p$, and $\zeta \in L$ a primitive $p$-th root of unity. Let $K$ be the intermediate field of $L \subseteq \mathrm{LaurentSeries}\,L$ obtained by adjoining to $L$ the coefficientwise image $\mathrm{coeffEmb}\,L$ of the $q$-expansion function field `x1FunctionField (M * p)` of $\Gamma_1(Mp)$ over $\mathbb{Q}$. Let $A$ be a discrete valuation domain with fraction field $L$ such that $p$ lies in the maximal ideal of $A$ and $\zeta$ lies in the image of $A$, with a compatible $A$-algebra structure on $K$, and let $\varpi \in A$ generate the maximal ideal. Let $j \in K$ be an element whose image in $\mathrm{LaurentSeries}\,L$ is the coefficientwise image of the $q$-expansion $\mathrm{jq} = q^{-1}\cdot \mathrm{jNumQ}$, with $j \neq 0$, and write $B = \mathrm{chartAlgFin}\,A\,K\,j$ for the subalgebra of $K$ of elements integral over $A[j]$, with $\mathrm{jChartFin}$ the element $j$ of $B$. Let $y \subset B$ be a prime ideal containing the image of $\varpi$, and assume the ideal generated by that image has two distinct minimal primes $\mathfrak{p} \neq \mathfrak{p}'$, both contained in $y$. Then for every algebraically closed field $\Omega$ of characteristic $p$ and every ring homomorphism $\varphi : B \to \Omega$ with kernel $y$, the value $\varphi(j)$ lies in $\mathrm{ssJSet}\,p\,\Omega$: every elliptic Weierstrass curve $W$ over $\Omega$ with $j$-invariant $\varphi(j)$ has no nonzero point $P$ of its affine model with $p \cdot P = 0$.
--
--   This is the implication "crossing point $\Rightarrow$ supersingular $j$-invariant" for the two-chart integral model of $X_1(Mp)$ over $A$, stated at the level of the finite chart ring: a prime of the special fibre lying on two distinct irreducible components has supersingular $j$-invariant, in the sense that the reduced curve has no $p$-torsion. It is the converse direction of the corresponding statement about minimal primes over $\varpi$, and is used to obtain the scheme-theoretic form of the assertion about the local rings of the integral model, the $\Gamma_1$-level analogue of the corresponding $\Gamma_0(Mp)$ statement being invoked after descent along the diamond operators.
